{"task_id": "Mbpp/261", "solution": "def division_elements(tuple1, tuple2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Performs element-wise division on two tuples.\n    Returns a new tuple with integer results where applicable.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(tuple1) != len(tuple2):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input tuples must have the same length.\")\n\n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(tuple1)):\n        dividend = tuple1[i]\n        divisor = tuple2[i]\n\n        if not isinstance(dividend, (int, float)) or not isinstance(divisor, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(\"Tuple elements must be numeric.\")\n\n        if divisor == 0:\n            raise ZeroDivisionError(\"Cannot divide by zero.\")\n        \n        division_result = dividend / divisor\n        if division_result == int(division_result):\n            result.append(int(division_result))\n        else:\n            result.append(division_result)\n            \n    return tuple(result)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/259", "solution": "```python\ndef maximize_elements(tuple1: tuple, tuple2: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Maximize corresponding elements between two tuples.\n    Each tuple contains numeric pairs, and the function returns element-wise maximums.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    def _validate_input(t1: tuple, t2: tuple) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validate input tuples for correct structure and length.\n        Ensures both tuples have the same length and elements are pairs.\n        \"\"\"\n        if len(t1) != len(t2):\n            return False\n        for i in range(len(t1)):\n            if not (isinstance(t1[i], tuple) and len(t1[i]) == 2 and\n                    isinstance(t2[i], tuple) and len(t2[i]) == 2):\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def _compare_pairs(pair1: tuple, pair2: tuple) -> tuple:\n        \"\"\"\n        Compare two tuple pairs and return maximized pair.\n        Returns a new pair where each element is the maximum of the corresponding inputs.\n        \"\"\"\n        return (max(pair1[0], pair2[0]), max(pair1[1], pair2[1]))\n\n    # Although validation is described, the problem statement implies valid inputs based on the assert.\n    # The design context indicates validation, but the final requirements state:\n    # \"Do NOT add isinstance() checks or raise exceptions for edge cases\"\n    # Therefore, assuming valid inputs based on typical competitive programming context\n    # where test cases adhere to implied constraints.\n    \n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(tuple1)):\n        maximized_pair = _compare_pairs(tuple1[i], tuple2[i])\n        result.append(maximized_pair)\n    \n    return tuple(result)\n```"}
