{"task_id": "Mbpp/265", "solution": "def list_split(input_list: list, n: int) -> list[list]:\n    \"\"\"Splits a list for every nth element, returning a list of the resulting sublists.\n    Each sublist contains elements where their original index modulo n matches the sublist's index.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Initialize n empty sublists. Assumes n is a positive integer.\n    result = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # Iterate through the input list and append each element to the appropriate sublist.\n    for i, item in enumerate(input_list):\n        result[i % n].append(item)\n\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/260", "solution": "def _newman_shanks_williams_number(k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the k-th Newman-Shanks-Williams number using a recurrence relation.\"\"\"\n    if k == 0:\n        return 1\n    if k == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    n_k_minus_2 = 1  # Corresponds to N_0\n    n_k_minus_1 = 1  # Corresponds to N_1\n\n    for _ in range(2, k + 1):\n        # N_k = 2 * N_{k-1} + N_{k-2}\n        current_n = 2 * n_k_minus_1 + n_k_minus_2\n        n_k_minus_2 = n_k_minus_1\n        n_k_minus_1 = current_n\n    \n    return n_k_minus_1\n\ndef _is_prime(num: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime using trial division.\"\"\"\n    if num < 2:\n        return False\n    if num == 2:\n        return True\n    if num % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= num:\n        if num % i == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 2\n    return True\n\ndef newman_prime(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the Newman-Shanks-Williams prime number at the specified index n.\n    This function returns N_n, assuming N_n is prime for the given n,\n    in accordance with the design context and test case `newman_prime(3) == 7`.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Calculate the n-th Newman-Shanks-Williams number.\n    nsw_number = _newman_shanks_williams_number(n)\n    \n    # As per the design, verify if the number is prime.\n    # The problem implies N_n will be prime for valid inputs of n.\n    if _is_prime(nsw_number):\n        return nsw_number\n    # If N_n is not prime, this case contradicts the problem statement\n    # (\"find the nth ... prime number\") and the implicit contract from the assertion.\n    # Given constraints, we assume valid 'n' inputs always result in a prime N_n.\n    # For robustness, could return nsw_number even if not prime (violating 'prime number' spec),\n    # but the design dictates a prime check, which we assume passes for expected inputs.\n    return nsw_number"}
