{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "def check_Consecutive(numbers: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given list contains consecutive numbers.\n    The list must contain at least two unique numbers to be considered consecutive.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return False\n\n    # Remove duplicates and sort the numbers\n    # Converting to a set first handles non-numeric types gracefully if they are unhashable,\n    # but based on \"no isinstance checks or raise exceptions\", we assume valid number types.\n    unique_numbers = sorted(list(set(numbers)))\n\n    # An empty list or a list with only one unique number cannot be consecutive\n    if len(unique_numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Check if each number is one greater than the previous number\n    for i in range(1, len(unique_numbers)):\n        if unique_numbers[i] != unique_numbers[i-1] + 1:\n            return False\n\n    return True"}
